#PMM(Physical Memory Manager):

In the operating system, the memory allocation and management is very important ---> where after the ***PMM*** only the kernel understands

##"thsi RAM belongs to me" or "this RAM is free" or "this RAM is never be touched"

#Bee-Kernel Implementation:

##Safe pHysical frame allocation:

###where the kernel asks - "give me one physical page" -- PMM returns - "one physical page"

##Frame release:

###where the PMM says to kernel "memory no longer needed" when the memory is free

##Memory awareness:

###By using memory awareness, kernel knows - ***total RAM, used RAM, free RAM, reserved RAM***

#Logic:

##divide RAm into equal frames, eg: divide RAM into = 4kb Frames

##1 Bit = 1 Frame, eg: 0 - free , 1 - used

###Bee-kernel uses - shell cmds to access the PMM info, like ***meminfo***, ***alloc***, ***free***
###meminfo - it tells the  ***total RAM, used RAM, free RAM, reserved RAM*** info's
###alloc - it allocates the frames (actually one frame)
###free - it free's the one free per cmd
